course: dictionary
lesson: 11331
title: An old word: yonder
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some words are old, and you meet them mostly in old books, poems, songs and films. «And yonder comes the famous Mr.» The word yonder tells you where something is: over there, in the distance, where you can still see it.
explain: Yonder is an old and country word. You might hear it in old poems, in the Bible, in cowboy films, or in «the wild blue yonder», which means the open sky. People still use it in parts of the southern USA and in rural Britain and Ireland.
explain: When you meet an unknown old word, use the clues around it. In «over yonder», the word over tells you that yonder is a place, not a feeling. If you swap in over there and the sentence still makes sense, you have found its meaning.
fact: Yonder means over there, at some distance but still within sight.
fact: Yonder can also mean that over there, as in «yonder tree», which means that tree over there.
fact: Yonder is an old word, found in poems, songs, old books and cowboy films.
fact: «The wild blue yonder» means the open sky, far away above you.
fact: Yonder is still heard in parts of the southern USA and in rural Britain and Ireland.
exercise: «Look at the hill yonder.» What does yonder mean? | Underground, out of sight | Long ago in the past | *Over there, still in sight | Very close, right by your feet
exercise: «We walked on to yonder tree.» What does yonder tree mean? | A tree in your own back garden | A tree that has been cut down | *That tree over there | A tree that is very tall and thin
exercise: «The plane flew off into the wild blue yonder.» What does the wild blue yonder mean? | A map of the sea | *The open sky | A blue lake far away | A kind of cake
test: «Over yonder, a farmer was calling his sheep.» Where is the farmer? | Inside the farmhouse, right next to you | Under the sea | *Over there, in sight but some way off | Behind the closed door
test: Which word is an old word that means over there? | Yesterday | Gently | *Yonder | Quickly
exam: Which sentence uses yonder correctly? | I ate my lunch yonder. | Yonder is my favourite colour. | We yonder to the shop after school. | *The village lies yonder, beyond the river.
